>\begin{enumerate}
>\item In the first year, Carlos plants the tree, but since it produces no lemons, he still has to pay the $3 for watering and feeding the tree. So his total cost for the first year is $90 (tree) + $3 (watering & feeding) = $93.
>\item Starting from the second year, the tree starts producing lemons. Over the course of the year, it grows 7 lemons, which are worth $1.5 each, for a total of $10.5. But remember, he also has to water and feed the tree, which costs $3. So his total income in the second year is $10.5 (lemons) - $3 (watering & feeding) = $7.5.
>\item After subtracting the cost of the second year ($93 + $3 = $96), he makes a profit of $7.5 - $96 = -$88.5, which is a loss.
>\item Going forward, however, once he breaks even, he starts earning money. Since the tree costs $96 to maintain for the first 2 years, he needs to make at least $96 in profit before that. But since each year he makes an additional $7.5 (after covering watering & feeding costs), we count backward from $96 by $7.5's:

> \boxed{\text{number of years} = \frac{96}{7.5} = \boxed{12.8} years}

>\end{enumerate}

Since you can't plant a tree for a fraction of a year, we round up to the nearest whole number, so it will take \boxed{13} years before Carlos starts earning money on the lemon tree.

